      <section id="what-happened" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="The lineup changed" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 14, Macklemore announced that he had been removed from eight scheduled appearances on Ed Sheeran's stadium tour, the Loop Tour. Macklemore said Sheeran and his team made the decision; tour promoter Messina Touring Group said venues had told it they would not allow concerts with Macklemore on the lineup.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed Sheeran and his team have made the decision to remove me from The Loop tour." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates that they will not allow a concert to take place with Macklemore on the lineup" /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">The dispute reaches beyond one lineup because three parties can shape what a stadium audience hears: a venue can refuse an act, a promoter pays the performers and deals with venues, and the headliner generally has substantial approval over the show.<Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="The promoter puts up the money, pays all of the acts, deals with the venue." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Certainly they can lock the doors" /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="at the end of the day that superstar headliner is going to have approval over everything" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">At MetLife Stadium, Macklemore called for a free Palestine, showed images of destruction in Gaza and performed “Hind's Hall.” The song was written after the 2024 campus protests, refers to Columbia University and is named for Hind Rajab, a five-year-old Palestinian girl killed in Gaza.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="He also showed images from the devastation in Gaza and performed his protest song, “Hind’s Hall,” about pro-Palestine demonstrators at Columbia" /><Cite s="ap-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Written following the 2024 campus protests in support of Palestinians, it’s named for Hind Rajab, a 5-year-old Palestinian girl who was killed after Israeli forces fired on her family’s car in Gaza City." /></p>

			        <p data-layer="narrative">Pink, a singer, reposted an Instagram message from StopAntisemitism, a pro-Israel campaign group, that accused Macklemore of ambushing fans; she later said she had not asked for him to be silenced or fired.<Cite s="bbc-macklemore-dropped-2026-09-15" passage="the campaign group StopAntisemitism said the musician had ambushed fans with propaganda - a claim that was amplified by the pop star Pink, who reposted it on Instagram." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="I have never asked for another artist to be silenced and I’m not asking now. I don’t need anyone fired, and I don’t need a refund." /> Macklemore said, “I am not a victim. Ed Sheeran is not a victim. Pink is not a victim,” and said all three still had careers, money, safety and audiences around the world.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="I am not a victim. Ed Sheeran is not a victim. Pink is not a victim." /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="We have careers, money, opportunities, safety and audiences around the world." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran's official North American schedule listed {seriesReceipt.sourceRows} shows, with {seriesReceipt.remainingShows} after September 14.<Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" /> Macklemore had been scheduled for eight of the later dates and had already appeared at both MetLife Stadium dates, on September 4 and 5.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Macklemore was slated to appear at eight of the 10 shows left" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="05 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="04 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 15, Finneas, Aaron Rowe, Lukas Graham and Beoga announced that they were leaving.<Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="four of his supporting acts abruptly quit" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">AP asked three music-business participants who were not involved in Sheeran's tour how a stadium tour normally works. They said a promoter finances the production, pays the acts and deals with venues; a venue can refuse a lineup; and a stadium-level headliner generally has substantial approval over the show.<Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Neither Gary nor Waddell nor Serwin are involved in the Sheeran tour." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="The promoter puts up the money, pays all of the acts, deals with the venue." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Certainly they can lock the doors" /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="at the end of the day that superstar headliner is going to have approval over everything" /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran said Macklemore had asked to join the tour the previous year and he agreed because he respected him as an artist.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="Last year Macklemore asked to join my tour and I agreed, because I respect him as an artist." /> He said Macklemore, like all his support acts, chose his own setlist.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="As with all my support acts, I agree that they play a setlist of their choosing." /> Sheeran said venues threatened to pull shows after Macklemore's performances because of how part of his message was conveyed. He spent the week speaking with them, including Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and Gillette Stadium, but could not change the venue and promoter decision.<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and their stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="After Macklemore's performances, venues for my upcoming tour dates communicated that they would pull the shows if he was still supporting. This was based on how a part of his message was conveyed." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="I spoke with the venues at length all week to try to build a bridge, including with Robert Kraft, but the venue's and promoter's decision was final." /> Sheeran said Macklemore's contract was with the promoter, not him.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="Macklemore's contract for the tour was with the promoter, not me." /> He said he would not abandon fans, crew, support acts or musicians who relied on him for work, and that his audience included children and families who did not expect a political forum.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="I would never let down the fans who had made plans, nor would I abandon the touring crew and the other support acts and musicians who rely on me for work and to make a living." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="My audience includes children and families, and they do not expect a political forum when they come to my shows." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["Date", "City", "Venue"]} rows={remainingDateRows} caption="Sheeran's 10 remaining North American dates after September 14" />

        <SourcedBlock source="loop-tour-north-america-faq" kind="record" detail>
			          <p>Ticket sales began in September 2025. The tour schedule still listed {seriesReceipt.sourceRows} North American shows on September 20. Ten of those dates fall after September 14.<Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="Artist Presale, powered by Seated      Sept. 23, 2025 at 9:00 AM" /><Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="September 19, 2026    Philadelphia, PA" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" kind="record" detail>
          <p>MetLife Stadium hosted Sheeran and Macklemore on September 4 and September 5.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="05 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="04 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="performing two shows at MetLife Stadium outside of New York City, on Sept. 4 and 5." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="why-removed" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="MetLife to venue objections" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why Macklemore was removed</h2>

			        <p data-layer="narrative">The first performance drew criticism from pro-Israel groups. After that show, the Israeli American Council, an advocacy group, launched a petition calling for Macklemore's removal. It addressed Sheeran, tour leadership and every venue hosting the tour, and asked whether his speech, visuals and “Hind's Hall” had been approved in advance.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="This spurred much criticism and backlash from pro-Israel groups." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="the Israeli American Council launched a petition demanding Sheeran remove Macklemore from the rest of the tour" /><Cite s="iac-loop-tour-petition-2026-09" passage="We call on Ed Sheeran, tour leadership, and the leadership of all venues hosting the Tour to:" /><Cite s="iac-loop-tour-petition-2026-09" passage="We also call on tour leadership to clarify whether Macklemore's political speech, visual content, and performance of Hind's Hall were approved in advance" /></p>

			        <p data-layer="narrative">The American Jewish Committee, an advocacy group that criticized the performance, said its New Jersey office contacted MetLife Stadium about the show. AJC said stadium administrators raised its concerns directly with the tour and artists and offered refunds before the second show to ticket holders who chose not to attend.<Cite s="ajc-metlife-intervention-2026-09-08" passage="AJC New Jersey immediately contacted the Meadowlands to express our community’s outrage." /><Cite s="ajc-metlife-intervention-2026-09-08" passage="The stadium administration raised these concerns directly with the tour and artists, and offered refunds to ticket holders before the second show who chose not to attend." /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">The Wall Street Journal reported, citing an unnamed person, that representatives of several NFL owners contacted the promoters after the first show and “heard back that Macklemore would leave out any mention of the issue from his act.” The Journal did not say who gave the assurance.<Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="Representatives of several NFL owners reached out to the promoters" /><Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="They heard back that Macklemore would leave out any mention of the issue from his act, the person said." /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">At the second MetLife show, Macklemore again called for a free Palestine. He addressed “all of my Jewish brothers and sisters,” said criticism of Israel and apartheid and being against genocide were not criticism of them, and said his message was peace, love, dignity, respect and equality.<Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="I want to say this to all of my Jewish brothers and sisters. Criticism of Israel, criticism of apartheid, being against genocide in no way is a criticism of you." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="At the second MetLife show, Macklemore reiterated his calls for a “Free Palestine” and addressed Jewish members of the audience" /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="My message is for peace; love for all human beings to be treated with dignity, respect, and equality." /></p>

			        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>CBS New York reported no response from MetLife Stadium to its request by September 7; no statement from the stadium appeared in the venue statements and coverage dated through September 20.<Cite s="cbs-metlife-response-2026-09-07" passage="CBS News New York also reached out to MetLife stadium for comment and did not hear back." /></p>

			        <p data-layer="narrative">Robert Kraft, the Gillette Stadium owner, confirmed that the venue had refused Macklemore. He said allowing Macklemore to appear on September 25 and 26 would make the venue a “platform for hate speech.”<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and their stadium" /><Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="do not provide a platform for hate speech" /><Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="we determined that his participation in the September 25th and 26th concerts at Gillette Stadium would cross that line" /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">Kraft cited Macklemore's recent actions, “material shared from the stage during Ed Sheeran's shows in New Jersey, and a broader history of antisemitic rhetoric and imagery.” He said the decision was not intended to diminish innocent Palestinians' suffering or deny anyone the right to advocate for them, and that such advocacy should not obscure Hamas's responsibility.<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="Macklemore’s recent actions, material shared from the stage during Ed Sheeran’s shows in New Jersey, and a broader history of antisemitic rhetoric and imagery" /><Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="This decision is not about diminishing the suffering of innocent Palestinians or denying anyone the right to advocate on their behalf." /><Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="But that advocacy should not come at the expense of the Jewish community or obscure the responsibility of Hamas" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said Sheeran maintained an “I don't take sides” position. He said he had made the same statements on stages for almost three years and argued that their association with a larger artist, major U.S. stadiums and bigger audiences made them newly risky.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'he couldn\'t get past his public facing, "I don\'t take sides."'} /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="What I said at MetLife I have been saying on stages around the world for almost three years." /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Because now I'm sharing a stage with one of the biggest artists in the world, in some of the biggest stadiums in America." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said antisemitism was real, but argued that criticism of Israel or opposition to Zionism should not be conflated with hatred of Jewish people.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Antisemitism is real." /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'When criticizing Israel, opposing Zionism or saying "Free Palestine" gets conflated with hatred of Jewish people'} /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said Sheeran told him that Kraft had rallied other stadium owners. He also said Sheeran told him that many people Sheeran had spoken with found “Free Palestine” and the Palestinian flag hurtful.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed also told me that Kraft had rallied some of the other stadium owners" /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'He told me that the words "Free Palestine" and the image of the Palestinian flag were hurtful to a lot of people he spoke with.'} /></p>

			        <p data-layer="narrative">In reporting Messina's claim that venues had objected, Rolling Stone named seven alleged objectors: Gillette Stadium, Raymond James Stadium, Lincoln Financial Field, Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Lucas Oil Stadium, Bank of America Stadium and AT&T Stadium.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="The venues that allegedly objected to the rapper include: Lincoln Financial Field in Philadelphia; Gillette Stadium in Foxborough, Massachusetts; Mercedes-Benz Stadium in Atlanta; Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis; Bank of America Stadium in Charlotte, North Carolina; AT&T Stadium in Arlington, Texas; and Raymond James Stadium in Tampa, Florida." /> Alan Clendenin, a Tampa Sports Authority board member and Tampa City Council chair, said the lineup change had nothing to do with the authority, which manages the county-owned Raymond James Stadium.<Cite s="tampa-bay-times-raymond-james-response-2026-09-15" passage={'One member, Tampa City Council chairperson Alan Clendenin, said the change "doesn\'t have anything to do with the Tampa Sports Authority."'} /><Cite s="tampa-raymond-james-ownership-timeline-2026-09-20" passage="Tampa Sports Authority conveys ownership of Raymond James Stadium® to Hillsborough County but retains responsibilities of managing the facility." /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Rolling Stone identified Bank of America Stadium and Gillette Stadium as privately owned.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Bank of America Stadium in Charlotte and Gillette Stadium in Foxborough" /> Official records show that public bodies own four of the other stadiums, while Philadelphia owns the land under Lincoln Financial Field: Georgia's stadium authority owns Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Indiana's stadium authority owns Lucas Oil Stadium, Arlington owns AT&T Stadium, and Hillsborough County owns Raymond James Stadium.<Cite s="gwcca-mercedes-benz-stadium-ownership-2026-09-20" passage="MBS, owned by the GWCCA, but operated and managed by the Atlanta Falcons/Arthur Blank organization" /><Cite s="indiana-lucas-oil-stadium-authority-2026-09-20" passage="as a new agency of the state to finance, design, construct and own the Lucas Oil Stadium" /><Cite s="arlington-att-stadium-lease-2026-04-21" passage="The team's lease of the City-owned stadium" /><Cite s="tampa-raymond-james-ownership-timeline-2026-09-20" passage="Tampa Sports Authority conveys ownership of Raymond James Stadium® to Hillsborough County but retains responsibilities of managing the facility." /><Cite s="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" passage="the City leases to the Authority a parcel of land in connection with the development of a new football stadium for the Eagles." /> The Falcons organization operates Mercedes-Benz Stadium, the Cowboys operate AT&T Stadium, and public boards operate Lucas Oil Stadium and Raymond James Stadium.<Cite s="gwcca-mercedes-benz-stadium-ownership-2026-09-20" passage="operated and managed by the Atlanta Falcons/Arthur Blank organization" /><Cite s="arlington-att-stadium-lease-2026-04-21" passage="The team will continue to operate and maintain the stadium complex" /><Cite s="indiana-lucas-oil-stadium-authority-2026-09-20" passage="it is leased and operated by the Capital Improvement Board of Managers of Marion County" /><Cite s="tampa-raymond-james-ownership-timeline-2026-09-20" passage="retains responsibilities of managing the facility" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Earlier that summer, Raymond James Stadium had booked a different artist under terms that FOX 13 reported limited the stadium authority's power to cancel. The outlet said the contract for two Kanye West concerts “explicitly blocks” the Tampa Sports Authority from canceling or interfering because of the artist's identity, past statements or political views, and that the promoter had requested the clause. It also reported that West's team accepted lawsuit liability and the promoter agreed to pay more than $2 million in stadium expenses plus $5 for each ticket sold; the authority collected those fees while West's team kept the remaining revenue, which could exceed $20 million.<Cite s="fox13-raymond-james-west-contract-2026-06-18" passage="The 38-page contract features a specific clause that explicitly blocks the TSA from canceling or interfering based on the artist's identity, past public statements or political viewpoints. West's promoter, Ikon Global, requested the clause." /><Cite s="fox13-raymond-james-west-contract-2026-06-18" passage="In exchange, West's team holds all liability if a lawsuit follows. Financially, the promoter pays more than $2 million to cover stadium expenses like cleaning and security, plus $5 for each of the 72,000 tickets sold. While the TSA collects these fees, West's team keeps the remaining revenue, which could exceed $20 million." /> Venue refusals can lead a promoter to remove an act from a national tour, but no public Sheeran-tour contract shows whether Macklemore had a similar clause or who made the final decision.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="After discussions with stakeholders, Macklemore will not be performing on the remaining support dates." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="If you’re paying for everything, yes you can make that call" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Wall Street Journal reported, citing unnamed sources, that Carolina Panthers owner David Tepper, Atlanta Falcons owner Arthur Blank and Philadelphia Eagles owner Jeffrey Lurie joined the pressure.<Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="More than that, several other National Football League owners were also ready to cancel Sheeran’s dates as long as Macklemore remained on the bill." /><Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="David Tepper and Arthur Blank" /><Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="Philadelphia Eagles owner Jeffrey Lurie would soon reach the same conclusion" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Who held final authority remains unresolved.</h3>
              <p>Macklemore attributed the removal to Sheeran and his team. Sheeran attributed it to the promoter and said the venues and promoter made the final decision. Messina said venues would not accept the lineup.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed Sheeran and his team have made the decision to remove me from The Loop tour." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="Macklemore coming off tour was the promoter's decision, it was not mine." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The four departures covered different tour roles.</h3>
              <p>AEG's schedule billed Rowe and Lukas Graham alongside Macklemore on all eight of his remaining dates. After his removal, they would have been the remaining advertised support on those dates. Beoga described itself as Sheeran's band for the remaining U.S. tour. Finneas was scheduled for South America later in the year.<Cite s="aeg-loop-tour-support-schedule-2026-09-21" passage="Support: Macklemore, Lukas Graham, Aaron Rowe" /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Macklemore was slated to appear at eight of the 10 shows left" /><Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="depart as Ed Sheeran's band on the remaining US Loop tour" /><Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="Finneas, who is Billie Eilish’s brother and musical collaborator, was due to tour with Sheeran in South America later this year" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="why-others-left" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="Four decisions, four roles" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why the other artists left</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Finneas said artists should not be silenced when they speak for oppressed people and withdrew from his upcoming dates with Sheeran.<Cite s="finneas-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="Artists must not be silenced when they speak up for the oppressed. I have decided to withdraw from my upcoming tour dates with Ed Sheeran." /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Aaron Rowe withdrew as a North American support act. He said he could not accept billionaires using their power to silence people who speak up.<Cite s="aaron-rowe-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="I have made the decision to withdraw as a support act for Ed Sheeran's The Loop North American Tour." /><Cite s="aaron-rowe-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="I cannot stand by and allow billionaires to use their position of power to silence the rightful voices of those who speak up" /></p>

	        <SourcedBlock source="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>Rowe later said the criticism should focus on Kraft rather than Sheeran, while maintaining that Macklemore should not have been removed.<Cite s="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" passage="when you really should be focusing on the likes of [New England Patriots owner] Robert Kraft." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Rowe affirmed that he didn’t think Macklemore should’ve been pulled off the tour" /></p>
	        </SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Beoga departed as Sheeran's band for the remaining U.S. dates. The band said it would not play venues that silence artists for saying “Free Palestine.”<Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="We have made the decision to depart as Ed Sheeran's band on the remaining US Loop tour" /><Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="we will not play in venues that silence artists for saying Free Palestine" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Beoga blamed what it called “zionist lobbies” for Macklemore's removal and told people to research Kraft, his relationship with Israel and the Boycott, Divestment and Sanctions movement.<Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="following the silencing of Macklemore by zionist lobbies" /><Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="We encourage everyone to research Robert Kraft, his relationship with Israel and the Boycott, Divestment and Sanctions movement." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Lukas Graham objected to wealth determining who gets to speak and withdrew from the remaining dates. The band also said there was not one single reason for its decision.<Cite s="lukas-graham-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="Nobody's bank balance should decide who gets to speak or which suffering we're allowed to acknowledge." /><Cite s="lukas-graham-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="We're withdrawing from the remaining dates. There isn't one single reason behind this decision." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["Artist", "Scheduled role", "What they announced"]} rows={lineupRows} caption="What each artist announced on September 15; AP described Rowe and Lukas Graham as Macklemore's would-be replacements" />

	      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="Philadelphia" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 17, the Philadelphia chapter of the Council on American-Islamic Relations, a civil-rights advocacy group known as CAIR-Philadelphia, announced that it had submitted a Pennsylvania Right-to-Know request concerning the Philadelphia lineup decision.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="The Philadelphia chapter of the Council on American-Islamic Relations (CAIR-Philadelphia) today announced that it has submitted a Pennsylvania Right-to-Know Law request seeking records concerning the decision" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">CAIR-Philadelphia said its request did not presume what any records would show. Pennsylvania gives an agency five business days after it receives a request to respond.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="the request does not presume what those records will show" /><Cite s="pa-open-records-citizens-guide-2026-09-20" passage="The clock starts the day after the RTK request is received during regular business hours. In other words, an agency has five business days to respond to a request" /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the Massachusetts attorney general's office told Boston 25 it was reviewing at least 25 complaints requesting refunds from Ticketmaster. The office said lineup changes do not necessarily entitle ticket purchasers to a refund.<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="they’re reviewing at least 25 complaints requesting refunds from Ticketmaster" /><Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="changes to the lineup do not necessarily entitle ticket purchasers to a refund" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Ticketmaster told Boston 25 that organizers set refund policy. The tour FAQ says, “All sales are final.”<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="refund policy is up to the organizers" /><Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="All sales are final." /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 19 Philadelphia show went ahead, and Sheeran began alone with no support act. He later introduced a new five-member backup band, made up of a drummer, guitarist, bass player, keyboardist and violinist, that had joined with three days' notice. He said he removed the secondary stage in the audience because he feared what could happen if he stood in the middle. A small group of pro-Palestinian demonstrators gathered outside before the concert.<Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="Sheeran began the concert alone on stage with no opener" /><Cite s="philadelphia-inquirer-loop-tour-review-2026-09-20" passage="He also introduced his new backup band, consisting of a drummer, guitarist, bass player, keyboardist, and violinist, who came on board on three days’ notice" /><Cite s="philadelphia-inquirer-loop-tour-review-2026-09-20" passage="I took the B stage out because I was afraid of what would happen if I stood in the middle" /><Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="Before Saturday’s show, a small group of pro-Palestinian demonstrators congregated outside the stadium." /> On September 20, Lincoln Financial Field's own event page still listed Macklemore, Lukas Graham and Aaron Rowe for the show and said it had last been changed on September 17.<Cite s="lincoln-field-loop-tour-2026-09-20" passage="will be joined on his stop at Lincoln Financial Field by Macklemore, Lukas Graham, and Aaron Rowe!" /><Cite s="lincoln-field-loop-tour-2026-09-20" passage={'dateModified":"2026-09-17T16:45:03+00:00'} /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">On stage in Philadelphia on September 19, Sheeran told the crowd, “I've had to take and make difficult decisions, and I am making mistakes, and I am so, so sorry.”<Cite s="philadelphia-inquirer-loop-tour-review-2026-09-20" passage="I’ve had to take and make difficult decisions, and I am making mistakes, and I am so, so sorry" /></p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran called the situation in Gaza “catastrophic and unjustifiable” and said he worried about venues preapproving performances.<Cite s="guardian-sheeran-philadelphia-video-2026-09-20" passage="catastrophic and unjustifiable" /><Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="He also told fans he worries about venues preapproving performances." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} />

		        <SourcedBlock source="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>CAIR-Philadelphia based its public-records request on the stadium's City-owned land: Philadelphia leases the parcel to its industrial-development authority, which leases all or part of the Eagles premises to the team.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="a major venue operating on publicly owned property" /><Cite s="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" passage="the City leases to the Authority a parcel of land in connection with the development of a new football stadium for the Eagles." /><Cite s="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" passage="the Authority will lease all or a portion of the Eagles Premises to the Eagles." /></p>
	        </SourcedBlock>
	        <SourcedBlock source="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>The Philadelphia show started two hours later than scheduled. Sheeran also called the October 7 attack horrific and said the injustice in the West Bank could not be overlooked.<Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="the show’s start time was pushed back two hours." /><Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="He called the Oct. 7 Hamas attack “horrific.”" /><Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="And the systemic injustice we’re seeing unfold in the West Bank, it cannot be overlooked." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ap-donations-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>On September 16, Macklemore pledged $1 million from his Loop Tour earnings to six Palestinian relief nonprofits. A Kraft spokesperson said Kraft and Sheeran had each agreed to donate $2 million, without specifying where the money would go.<Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="$1 million pledge from rapper Macklemore" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="Macklemore committed to donate his net earnings from Ed Sheeran’s Loop Tour" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="The six donation recipients" /><Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="a spokesperson for Kraft has said the billionaire and Sheeran have each agreed to donate $2 million but have not specified where the money will be directed" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="What remains private" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>As of September 20, no executed artist, promoter or venue contract was public.</p>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>Communications among Kraft, Messina, Sheeran's representatives and each venue, including those sought in CAIR-Philadelphia's request about the Philadelphia decision, were not public. It was not known whether Kraft coordinated broader venue pressure or who made the final decisions at Lincoln Financial Field, Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Lucas Oil Stadium, Bank of America Stadium and AT&T Stadium.</p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>No rebuilt support lineup for the September 25 through November 7 dates was public.</p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>This was the Loop Tour's first change to its support lineup on record.</p>

		        <p data-layer="narrative">Gillette Stadium's Ticketmaster terms say, “No refund will be owed if an opening act or festival performer is changed or canceled.” Massachusetts law separately requires licensed ticket sellers to guarantee a refund when a ticket fails to conform to its advertised description, unless the purchaser preapproved a substitution of tickets.<Cite s="gillette-ticketmaster-terms-2026-09-20" passage="No refund will be owed if an opening act or festival performer is changed or canceled." /><Cite s="massachusetts-ticket-refund-law-185d-2026-09-20" passage="the ticket fails to conform to its description as advertised unless the purchaser has pre-approved a substitution of tickets" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>The attorney general's September 18 statement did not say whether removing an advertised opening act met the law's ticket-description test.</p>
      </section>

